{"id":"57fda7e9-fdb6-4a40-a192-d3fd6385024f","object":"chat.completion","created":1785012549,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: missing nodes or required_symbols\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid types\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(node, dict) or 'id' not in node or 'symbol' not in node or 'removable' not in node or 'parent' not in node or 'depth' not in node:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid node structure\\\")\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node id must be string\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n    roots = [nid for nid, nd in node_map.items() if nd['parent'] is None]\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Must have exactly one root\\\")\\n    root_id = roots[0]\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for nid, nd in node_map.items():\\n        p = nd['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            if p not in children:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent {p} not found\\\")\\n            children[p].append(nid)\\n    symbol_set = set(nd['symbol'] for nd in node_map.values())\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbol_set:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol {sym} not present in tree\\\")\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for nd in node_map.values():\\n        sym = nd['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    removed_order = []\\n    nodes_sorted = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    for nid in nodes_sorted:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        nd = node_map[nid]\\n        if not nd['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        sym = nd['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) based on the provided description. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" for a parse tree. The input case has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. We need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain, such that required symbols are still present, and we remove nodes that are removable, depth-first? The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes from deepest to shallowest (descending depth). For each node that is removable, we consider removing it tentatively if all required symbols remain represented after removal. Also we cannot remove root (removable=false for root), cannot remove required symbols? Actually required_symbols is a list of symbols that must be present in the tree. So we need to ensure that after removal, for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. Additionally, we cannot remove nodes that have retained children? The description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove the node because that would orphan the children? In a tree, removing a node would remove its entire subtree. But the problem likely defines removal as a node being pruned (including its descendants). If we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed. So if we remove a node, we must ensure that no required symbols are lost. Also if a node has children that we decide to retain (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove the parent because that would remove the children. So the decision must be consistent: we process nodes bottom-up, and for each node we decide to remove it only if all its descendants are also being removed? Or we need to ensure that after removal, the children are also removed. The description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests that we should not remove a node if any of its children are retained. So when we process a node, we should check if its children (by parent ID) are among the retained set. If they are retained, then we cannot remove this node. Also, if we remove a node, all its descendants will be removed as well (since subtree is gone). So we must also ensure that we don't later try to remove a descendant that is already removed.\n\nThe problem resembles \"perses\" syntax-guided reduction. The goal: given a parse tree, remove as many nodes as possible (with highest utility? but utility is given but not used in the description? Possibly we need to consider utility? The description mentions \"utility\" but not used in decision rules. The instructions: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we need to sort nodes by depth descending, and for equal depth, by some order? The description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for ties in depth, we should order by node ID (lexicographically? likely string compare).\n\nWe need to output removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (probably ascending string order). removed_node_ids should be in the order we removed them (the actual removal order, i.e., the order in which we decided to remove nodes). Also we must include root and all nodes not removed.\n\nWe need to validate that the input is structurally valid. Raise ValueError if infeasible: for example, if required_symbols includes symbols not present at all, or if root is not present, etc.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Parse input case. Expect case to be a dict with \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings). Also \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n2. Build a dictionary from node ID to node dict.\n3. Build a set of all node IDs.\n4. Build parent-child mapping: for each node, add its ID to children list of its parent (if parent not None).\n5. Verify that root exists (parent None). If not, raise ValueError.\n6. Check that all required_symbols are present in at least one node's symbol. If not, raise ValueError.\n7. Determine which nodes are removable: nodes where removable is True.\n8. Initialize set of retained nodes to all nodes.\n9. Initialize list removed_order = [].\n10. Get list of node IDs sorted by depth descending, then by ID (ascending) for tie-breaking. (since deeper nodes first, then within same depth, by ID)\n11. For each node ID in that order:\n   - If node ID is not in retained set, skip (already removed by parent removal).\n   - If node is not removable, skip.\n   - If node has children that are in retained set (i.e., some children are not removed yet), then skip (cannot remove because retained children).\n   - Tentatively consider removing this node: compute if after removal of this node (and its entire subtree), all required symbols are still present in the remaining retained nodes (excluding this node and all its descendants). Since we process bottom-up, the descendants are either already removed or not yet processed? Actually if we process deeper nodes first, we will have already decided on descendants. If a descendant is retained, then its parent cannot be removed because the child would be removed. So if we have any retained children, we skip. If all children are removed (or no children), then we can consider removal.\n   - To check required symbols: we need to know which symbols are present in the remaining retained nodes after removal. We can precompute a mapping from symbol to count of nodes with that symbol in retained set. Then simulate removal: remove this node and all its descendants (but since we are processing bottom-up, descendants are either already removed or not retained? Actually if a descendant is retained, we would have skipped because children retained. So if we reach this point, all descendants are either not in retained set (i.e., already removed) or never existed. So we only need to subtract the counts for this node and its immediate children? But there could be grandchildren that are already removed, so their symbols are already subtracted. Simpler: compute the set of all nodes in the subtree rooted at this node (including itself) that are currently in retained set. Since we process bottom-up and we only consider removal when all children are not retained, the subtree's retained nodes are exactly this node (if it has no children) or only this node if children already removed. Actually if a child is retained, we would skip. So when we consider removal, the only retained node in the subtree is this node itself (because all descendants have been either removed or were never retained? But we might have removed some descendants earlier, but they are no longer in retained set. So the subtree's retained nodes are exactly this node. Therefore we just need to check if removing this node causes any required symbol to become missing. That is, we subtract 1 from the count of this node's symbol. If after subtraction, any required symbol count becomes 0, then we cannot remove.\n   - If removal is allowed:\n        - Remove this node from