ent root, children: n4 is removed, so no retained children? n4 removed, so no retained children. So check required symbols: removing n2 (symbol s2) not required, allowed. Remove n2, subtract s2. Removed_order: [n4, n6, n2]. Then n3 (removable false, skip). Depth 0 root skip. Retained set: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted IDs: n1, n3, n5, root. Output: {\"removed_node_ids\": [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"], \"retained_node_ids\": [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]}. That seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify with the required_symbols: after removal, s1 is still present (n1), root present. Good.\n\nSecond case similar but different utilities. Should produce similar removal pattern.\n\nNow we must write the solve function. Also handle invalid cases: e.g., if required_symbols contain symbol not present, raise ValueError. If nodes missing fields, raise. Also ensure that payload has \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Use only standard library. No random or network.\n\nImplementation in Python. Then wrap in JSON output with single key \"implementation\". Must escape newlines etc.\n\nAlso note: The instruction says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`.\" So the outer JSON will have key \"implementation\" and value is a string containing Python code. That string must be valid JSON-escaped.\n\nWe'll produce the code as a multi-line string, escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, double quotes as \\\". Also ensure no trailing spaces.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing nodes or required_symbols\")\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid types\")\n    # Build node map\n    node_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        if not isinstance(node, dict) or 'id' not in node or 'symbol' not in node or 'removable' not in node or 'parent' not in node or 'depth' not in node:\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid node structure\")\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node id must be string\")\n        node_map[nid] = node\n    # Find root(s)\n    roots = [nid for nid, nd in node_map.items() if nd['parent'] is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Must have exactly one root\")\n    root_id = roots[0]\n    # Build children map\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for nid, nd in node_map.items():\n        p = nd['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            if p not in children:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {p} not found\")\n            children[p].append(nid)\n    # Check all required symbols are present\n    symbol_set = set(nd['symbol'] for nd in node_map.values())\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in symbol_set:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not present in tree\")\n    # Initialize retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for nd in node_map.values():\n        sym = nd['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    removed_order = []\n    # Get list of node IDs sorted by depth descending, then by ID\n    nodes_sorted = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    for nid in nodes_sorted:\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        nd = node_map[nid]\n        if not nd['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child is retained\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        # Check if removal preserves required symbols\n        sym = nd['symbol']\n        # If removing this node would cause required symbol count to drop to zero, skip\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 1:\n            continue\n        # Also need to consider that removing this node also removes its entire subtree,\n        # but since we ensured no children are retained, the only node from subtree in retained is this node.\n        # So just decrement symbol count for this node.\n        # But careful: if there are grandchildren that were already removed, their symbol counts already subtracted.\n        # So we only need to handle this node.\n        # However, what if a child was removed earlier but its symbol remains counted in symbol_counts? \n        # That's fine because we subtracted when that child was removed. So symbol_counts is cumulative of retained nodes only.\n        # So we can proceed.\n        # Remove node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n    # Get retained sorted by ID\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n\nBut we must consider multiple children retained? The check \"any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\" covers that. Also we need to handle the case where a node has no children: children list empty, so no retained children.\n\nWe should also consider that removing a node might affect subtree nodes that are still retained? But we already ensured no children are retained. However, what about nodes deeper than children? They would be grandchildren. If a child is not retained (i.e., already removed), but its child (grandchild) might still be retained if we didn't process that grandchild yet? But we process top-down by depth descending, so deeper nodes are processed first. So grandchildren are processed before their parent. So if a grandchild is retained, then its parent (the child) would have a retained child, and when we later process the child, we would see retained child and skip. But we might have already processed the grandchild and decided to retain it. Then when we get to the child, we see retained child and skip. That's correct. If the grandchild is removed earlier, then the child has no retained grandchildren. So it's fine.\n\nNow implement as a function.\n\nEdge case: what if required_symbols includes a symbol that appears multiple times but after removals only one remains? That's fine. Our check only prevents removal if it would cause the count to drop to zero when the symbol is required.\n\nNow we need to produce the final JSON with the implementation string. We'll write the code as a triple-quoted string, but within JSON we must escape. We'll use code string with proper escaping.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4266,"total_tokens":6435,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3600},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}